Assignment 3 — Troubleshooting high load average on Linux server

#confirms extremely high load. The 3 numbers are 1, 5, 15-minute load averages.
uptime 

#Look at %CPU, %MEM, %wa (IO wait).
top or htop

#What to look for: one or few processes using 100% CPU; high %wa means I/O wait (disk stuck). Many processes in D state (uninterruptible sleep) indicate IO waits.
top press 1 to see per-CPU usage.

#Find top CPU consumers. If a single process incremental makes load high, note its PID and command.
ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head -n 20

#Look for memory hogs — heavy swapping also increases load.
ps aux --sort=-%mem | head -n 20

#What to look for: devices with very high %util (near 100%), high await, high svctm. That means disk is saturated.

iostat -x 1 5 (from sysstat package)

#Look at r (runnable processes) and b (blocked). High r = CPU bottleneck; high b or high wa shows I/O blocking. Check si/so for swapping.
vmstat 1 5

#Shows processes doing most IO right now. Look for processes with heavy READ/WRITE.
iotop -o (needs root)

#Kernel messages — look for disk errors, OOM killer, filesystem errors, driver failures.

dmesg | tail -n 50 & journalctl -xe

#See RAM, swap usage. If swap is heavily used, performance suffers and load increases.

free -m

#See established connections; look for network flood or many incoming connections (DDOS, slowloris).

ss -tunp / netstat -plant

#See files/sockets a process has open.
lsof -p <PID> (for suspicious PID)

#If a particular process is stuck, strace can show what system calls it’s waiting on (read, write, futex, open).

strace -p <PID> (careful, can slow process)

#For suspicious services (e.g., apache/nginx/mysql), check recent logs and restart history.

systemctl status <service> and journalctl -u <service>

#If it's a database server: check DB process stats  queries causing high CPU/IO, slow queries.

mysql SHOW PROCESSLIST;

######How to interpret common findings

High CPU usage: top will show processes with high %CPU. Action: investigate process, check if it's legitimate, consider restarting or killing, or scaling CPU.

High IO wait (%wa): iostat and iotop will show disk saturation — maybe due to backup, large writes, or failing disk. Action: stop heavy IO task, move to different disk, or scale to faster storage (EBS gp3).

High memory + swap: free -m and vmstat show swapping — leads to high load. Action: free memory (stop processes), add memory, tune caching.

Many processes in D state: usually IO blocking; could indicate filesystem or NFS hangs.

Large number of processes or many short-lived processes: could be fork-bomb or job scheduler misconfigured. Check process list and what launched them (cron, systemd timers).

Network flood: ss/netstat show many connections from same IPs — could be DoS. Action: block IPs with firewall, enable rate limiting.

Example remediation steps (once root cause known)

CPU-bound process: kill -9 <PID> or systemctl restart <service> (after risk assessment). Or scale vertically (bigger instance) or horizontally (add instances behind a load balancer).

Disk saturated: stop/limit the job, move logs to different disk, increase IOPS (in AWS switch to faster EBS type), check failing disk.

Memory exhaustion: stop services, tune caches, add swap temporarily, add RAM (resize instance).

Network attack: block at firewall (iptables, security group), enable AWS WAF / AWS Shield if in cloud.

Application bug: roll back recent deploy; review app logs.





